> where the error occurs.  It looks like the error.check( ) function would
> be ideal, but I cannot get it to work as in the example in Chambers'
> book 'Programming with Data' (which is really the only reference I can
> find for such a function).
>
>
>
> I can get a 'F' but cannot get a 'T'.
>
>
>
>  > echk1 <- c(9, 16, 25)
>  > sqrt(echk1)
> [1] 3 4 5
>  > error.check(sqrt(echk1))
> [1] F
>
>  > echk2 <- c(9, "h", 25)
>  > sqrt(echk2)
> Problem in x^0.5: Non-numeric first operand
> Use traceback() to see the call stack
>  > error.check(sqrt(echk2))
> [1] "Error action not carried out"
>
> According to the book, I should get a 'T' for the second test above with
> the explanation of non-numeric operand.  Instead it looks like the error
> action is simply not carried out.
>
>
>
> Any thoughts would be greatly appreciated.

How about:

echk2 <- list(9, "h", 25) # note change from `c()'
pass <- vector("logical", 3)
for(i in 1:3) {
  inv <- try(sqrt(echk2[[i]]))
  pass[i] <- data.class(inv) != "Error"
}

