Micha,

 

Have you got chance to solved your question 
yet?   There is a function called "julian" in Splus (Try ?julian at 
Splus prompt to find out more details).  Here is the usage: julian(m, d, y, origin=c(month=1, day=1, year=1960)) 

 

Suppose a is the vector of dates you have, 
with the dates in yyyy/mm/dd, you can use the following codes

to get the julian dates.

 

y_as.numeric(substring(a,1,4))

m_as.numeric(substring(a,6,7))

d_as.numeric(substring(a,9,10))

juliandatesofa_julian(m=m,d=d,y=y)    # Assume the origin 
date is default 01/01/1960.

 

The output will be:     [1] 14825 14826 14828 14832 14852 14880 14908 
14936 14971 14992 14999

 

Hope this helps.

 

